-- task-graph/hyperactivity.cub — this work's nodes (lib/task-graph.cub; the meaning and rules are docs/task-graph-design.md, read it
-- first). A node's definition gives its identity and the nodes it is a child of; nodes are defined
-- after their parents. A node's children always cover it completely; where they are not certainly
-- complete, one child is the node's residual. A goal's statement is the comment `name (date):` above
-- its definition. A node opened for a specific piece of work (named tN) has its text as the one
-- comment `tN (date):` at the place in the code it concerns (`python3 tools/task-graph.py show tN`), or here
-- below when it has no single place (residuals among them). Every node here is open: resolving one
-- removes it (and its text) and folds what matters into where it belongs; the history keeps it.
-- Loaded after lib/task-graph.cub.
--
-- t33 (2026-11-12): question. Not yet done: reading the evaluator's rule bodies (src/net.c's
-- interaction cases, src/dnf.c's face algebra) side by side with the published rules, CCHM (Cohen,
-- Coquand, Huber, Mörtberg, TYPES 2015, LIPIcs 69) and the clocked extension (Kristensen,
-- Møgelberg, Vezzosi, arXiv:2102.01969), each rule recorded as matching or deviating at its site.
-- The two papers are not in the repository; fetch them. Until this is done, conformance of the
-- implementation to the calculus is not known.
--
-- t34 (2026-11-12): question. Not yet done: reading src/lang.c's translation of terms into the
-- evaluator's graph for anything that can change a term's meaning (the strictness analysis that
-- decides which operands are built eagerly, fully lazy lifting, sweeping of compiled code, the
-- module cache, whose key is the build date and the files' texts and whose hits are trusted).
--
-- t35 (2026-11-12): question. Not yet done: reading lib/check.cub's typing clauses against the
-- calculus's typing rules, each recorded as matching or deviating at its site.
--
-- t74 (2026-11-13): residual of initial-deliverable: determine and do every essential (non-example)
-- construction of the paper that core and self-aware do not cover, each carved out as a part of
-- initial-deliverable or of the node it belongs to. Candidates
-- from reading the paper in full: §5.1's Language record, Lift, Trans and erase; §5.5's Signature,
-- Machine, Language, meaning, Property/Proof/Annotated, RuleSig and Adequate; §8.2's
-- SourceBeh/TargetBeh/Conforms/Correct; §9.3's realize_M, Block and seq (9.12–9.13), the compiler
-- algebra and its congruences (9.15–9.17); §10.1/§10.5's dependency certificates and producer
-- transitions; §10.8's distinction of digest, ≡ and SemEq.
--
-- t75 (2026-11-13): needs-work. The paper's worked examples as checked definitions (tracks/
-- PAPER-READING.md records each as absent): §5.1's intrinsically typed language and its
-- substitution theorem; §5.2's let elimination (5.4); §5.5's memory interpreter and its trace;
-- §5.6's cancel, cancelSound, preserve; (3.7) A ≃ Graph(f); §4's Vec ≃ (Fin → A) with Sorted
-- transported, Monoid, Package, graph relabeling; §6.3's semiring interpretations (6.11–6.13) and
-- Clauses/fold (6.14); §6.4's conservative extension (6.17–6.18); §9.3's stack machine (9.2–9.10),
-- the toy ISA trace (9.14) and the 46-byte decoder with χ_b; §8.3's encodeLE/decodeLE (8.6–8.12);
-- §10.3's credit conservation with receipts; §10.7's mixed-version invariant; Appendix A.
--
